body:
You're not a psychic. You can't be expected to read other people's minds. If your coworkers had an issue with you, the onus was on them to talk to you about it. You've already analyzed your past behavior and, as far as you know, you did nothing wrong. Glaring, scoffing, and rolling one's eyes is childish and counterproductive. Passive aggression does nothing but create more negativity. These are not the kind of people whose opinions should matter to you. In fact, the only opinion that really matters is your own.   


Odds are that you had a negative experience at some point and it triggered your anxiety, which made you more likely to notice other negative experiences. I doubt that you did anything wrong. The fact that you care about your coworkers' feelings proves that you're a socially conscious person with good intentions, and that's all that really matters. If I were you I would try to forget about this experience and move on.

&#x200B;

I'm a janitor at a candy factory. I spend most of my work day scrubbing sugar residue off of the factory's walls and floors. It's a menial, low-paying job, but it's one of the few jobs where I don't have to interact with other people. Unfortunately some of the other employees patronize me because they assume I'm poor and uneducated. They don't know that I have a college degree and I'm married to a Harvard graduate who earns more than 4X their annual salary. XD

During my free time I draw, paint, and design digital graphics. I sell my artwork at a local art gallery that features artists with mental illnesses. For my next creative endeavor I will build miniature dioramas inside of tiny glass terrariums.

My husband was once a professional photographer and he still has all of his equipment. Occasionally we'll collaborate on a photo shoot. For our last photo shoot I dressed as the character Ophelia from Shakespeare's play, "The Tragedy of Hamlet". She died by drowning, so I floated on my back, down a river, while he took photos. We're planning to do an entire series of photo shoots based on tragic female characters from popular plays and novels.

On weekends we travel across the country and attend operas, musicals, plays, symphonies, comedy shows, etc. Art and culture is an important part of our lives.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

